---
import ContactCTA from '../components/ContactCTA.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="About | Ivan Garcia Sainz-Aja"
	description="About Ivan Garcia Sainz-Aja and his work in Event-Driven Architecture, AsyncAPI, and Kafka platform design."
>
	<div class="stack gap-20">
		<main class="wrapper about">
			<Hero
				title="About"
				tagline="I help teams and organizations create software that is easy to understand at scale."
			>
				<img
					width="800"
					height="533"
					src="/SpringIO-2025_800x533.jpg"
					alt="Iván García Sainz-Aja at Spring I/O 2025"
				/>
			</Hero>

			<section>
				<h2 class="section-title">Background</h2>
				<div class="content">
					<p>
						I'm a programmer. I love learning, building, and sharing knowledge.
					</p>
					<p>
						I have been a technical leader for most of my career. I like to understand the why before writing any code: close enough to domain discovery to understand it, and technical enough to build the solution. I love sharing knowledge and mentoring engineers in their careers.
					</p>
					<p>
						I have deep hands-on experience designing API-First tooling and governance processes. That work brought me into the AsyncAPI community, where I became a Technical Steering Committee (TSC) member and Ambassador.
					</p>
					<p>
						Keeping APIs and the systems they describe in sync is more about automation than governance and policy. Only automation can create API-First governance processes that last.
					</p>
					<p>
						I have a deep understanding of the Spring and Spring Boot ecosystems. I've been using Spring Framework since version 1.0.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="section-title">ZenWave SDK</h2>
				<div class="content">
					<p>
						I built ZenWave SDK to help me automate software design and implementation, before agents were a thing.
					</p>
					<p>
						It's built around the idea of Domain-Driven Design as Code, with a parser and a set of templates, grown over the years, that help me build on known and proven patterns without copy-pasting previous work, skipping the boring, clerical work.
					</p>
					<p>
						I like mentoring, and ZenWave SDK helped junior teammates perform as seniors because the tool makes good architecture visible and accessible.
					</p>
					<p>
						I love creating software that is easy to
						understand, easy to maintain, and easy to evolve, because its blueprint is so visible
						it can be questioned, validated, and improved. Then I move on to the next
						interesting problem instead of repeating the same boring patterns.
					</p>
					<p>
						This is why I built and programmed every line of ZenWave SDK. You can find it at
						<a href="https://zenwave360.io" target="_blank">zenwave360.io</a>.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section>
	<h2 class="section-title">ZenWave Platform</h2>
	<div class="content">
		<p>
			ZenWave Platform started in the agentic era.
			It's built to help you edit and visualize ZenWave DSL models along with API-First specifications like OpenAPI and AsyncAPI.
		</p>
		<p>
			It also helps you visualize your API architecture as a connected graph.
		</p>
		<p>
			In most organizations, the architecture lives scattered and disconnected in different repos and sources, and tribal knowledge in people’s heads. ZenWave Architecture Manifest can bring together all those scatered sources into a graph navigable in your editor.
		</p>
		<p>
			It connects your existing <strong>AsyncAPI</strong> and <strong>OpenAPI</strong> specifications with <strong>ZenWave ZDL</strong> models into one navigable architecture model.
		</p>
		<p>
			The result is a living map of your system. Engineers, architects, and even AI agents can explore it, reason about it, and keep it consistent as the organization evolves.
		</p>
		<p>
			I'm building this, but I don't look at the code anymore. It has evolved from pure Java to Kotlin Multiplatform, so it can run on the JVM (IntelliJ), Node.js (VS Code), and in the browser (vscode.dev).
		</p>
	</div>
</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="section-title">Topics I speak and write about</h2>
				<div class="content">
					<p>
						Domain-Driven Design and Event-Driven Architectures. API-First development
						with OpenAPI and AsyncAPI. Event governance, contracts, and testing.
						Monoliths, moduliths, and microservices in Java and Spring.
						From model to code: turning domain conversations into running systems.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

		</main>

		<ContactCTA />
	</div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.about {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 3.5rem;
	}

	img {
		margin-top: 1.5rem;
		border-radius: 1.5rem;
		box-shadow: var(--shadow-md);
	}

	section {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		color: var(--gray-200);
	}

	.section-title {
		grid-column-start: 1;
		font-size: var(--text-xl);
		color: var(--gray-0);
	}

	.content {
		grid-column: 2 / 4;
	}

	.content :global(a) {
		text-decoration: 1px solid underline transparent;
		text-underline-offset: 0.25em;
		transition: text-decoration-color var(--theme-transition);
	}

	.content :global(a:hover),
	.content :global(a:focus) {
		text-decoration-color: currentColor;
	}

	@media (min-width: 50em) {
		.about {
			display: grid;
			grid-template-columns: 1fr 60% 1fr;
		}

		.about > :global(:first-child) {
			grid-column-start: 2;
		}

		section {
			display: contents;
			font-size: var(--text-lg);
		}

		.section-title {
			justify-self: end;
			padding-right: 0.25rem;
		}

		.content {
			grid-column: 2;
			max-width: 62ch;
		}
	}
</style>
